// Permit Login if a Login on server `s1` happened within the last
// hour. The predicate pins `server` to a *string literal* `"s1"`
// rather than to a context-bound value — this is the position that
// tripped the original trace-field-ordering bug, where the runtime
// driver rebuilt the trace event's named context by zipping the
// trace's positional arg values against the schema's alphabetized
// field names. With Login's input alphabetized to `(server, user)`
// but the trace writing `(user, server)`, the row's `server` slot
// ended up holding the user's value, and the comparison
// `server == "s1"` never fired even when the trace's `server` was
// in fact `"s1"`. `formerly within Nh` is reflexive (matches the
// current time-point), so both Login events should produce a
// permit verdict.
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Login",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.server: "s1" }
};
